-- title:
--   Child-average returns at the exact tree tolerance
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Consider the tree built by the candidate stages, with anchor $a$.
--
--   1. **Run tolerance.** The conservative scale $2^{-k}\beta(\mathrm{base}_k)$ used at stage $k$ is at most the retained-budget tolerance $\tau_k$.
--   2. **Child averages.** Every parent $p$ at level $k$ satisfies
--   $$
--   \Bigl\|\sum_{\mathrm{par}(c)=p}w(c)\,f_{k+1,c}(a)-a\Bigr\|\le\tau_k ,
--   $$
--   in the original normalized norm.
